<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p><italic>describing the high-level functionality and purpose of the
  software for a diverse, non-specialist audience</italic></p>
  <p><monospace>Pygraphon</monospace> is a Python library designed to
  estimate graphons, providing an intuitive interface for researchers
  and practitioners to model and analyze graph structures efficiently.
  Graphons are probability distributions on the space of graphs,
  allowing users to infer underlying network characteristics from
  observed data. This library caters to a broad audience, enabling
  non-specialists to engage with graphon estimation techniques and apply
  them in various fields, including social networks, neuroscience, and
  machine learning.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p><italic>clearly illustrates the research purpose of the software
  and places it in the context of related work.</italic></p>
  <p>Graphon estimation stands as a crucial tool in the analysis of
  large-scale networks, serving as a fundamental step in understanding
  complex systems. <monospace>Pygraphon</monospace> addresses the need
  for accessible, user-friendly tools that enable researchers across
  domains to estimate graphons accurately. By providing a comprehensive
  set of functionalities for graphon modeling, estimation, and
  visualization, this library contributes to the advancement of network
  science and facilitates insights into the structure and dynamics of
  complex networks.</p>
  <sec id="similar-software">
    <title>Similar software</title>
    <p><italic>A list of key references, including to other software
    addressing related needs. Note that the references should include
    full names of venues, e.g., journals and conferences, not
    abbreviations only understood in the context of a specific
    discipline.</italic></p>
    <list list-type="bullet">
      <list-item>
        <p><monospace>R</monospace> package
        <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://cran.r-project.org/package=graphon"><monospace>graphon</monospace></ext-link>
        (<xref alt="You, 2017" rid="ref-graphon" ref-type="bibr">You,
        2017</xref>)</p>
      </list-item>
      <list-item>
        <p><monospace>R</monospace> package
        <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://enigmasong.github.io/nethist/index.html"><monospace>nethist</monospace></ext-link>
        <italic>(development stage)</italic>
        (<xref alt="Song &amp; Olhede, 2023" rid="ref-song2023" ref-type="bibr">Song
        &amp; Olhede, 2023</xref>)</p>
      </list-item>
      <list-item>
        <p><monospace>Julia</monospace> package
        <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://docs.juliahub.com/General/NetworkHistogram/stable/"><monospace>NetworkHistogram.jl</monospace></ext-link>
        (<xref alt="Dufour &amp; Grainger, 2023" rid="ref-Dufour2023" ref-type="bibr">Dufour
        &amp; Grainger, 2023</xref>)</p>
      </list-item>
    </list>
    <p>We list the different methods implemented in
    <monospace>Pygraphon</monospace>, <monospace>graphon</monospace>,
    and <monospace>NetworkHistogram.jl</monospace> latter in Table
    2.</p>
    <p><italic>Mention (if applicable) a representative set of past or
    ongoing research projects using the software and recent scholarly
    publications enabled by it.</italic></p>
    <p>Recent Scholarly Publications Enabled by
    <monospace>Pygraphon</monospace>:</p>
    <list list-type="bullet">
      <list-item>
        <p>Verdeyme &amp; Olhede
        (<xref alt="2024" rid="ref-verdeyme2024shape" ref-type="bibr">2024</xref>)</p>
      </list-item>
    </list>
  </sec>
</sec>
<sec id="functionality-and-implementation">
  <title>Functionality and implementation</title>
  <p>For more details, please refer to the
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://pygraphon.readthedocs.io/en/latest/">documentation</ext-link>.</p>
  <p><disp-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[ A\_{ij} | \xi_i, \xi_j \sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(W(\xi_i, \xi_j)) ]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>A</mml:mi><mml:mi>_</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>i</mml:mi><mml:mi>j</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>ξ</mml:mi><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>ξ</mml:mi><mml:mi>j</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>∼</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">B</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">e</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">r</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">n</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">o</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">u</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">l</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">l</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>W</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>ξ</mml:mi><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>ξ</mml:mi><mml:mi>j</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula></p>
  <table-wrap>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Methods</th>
          <th>class names</th>
          <th>Reference</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Matrix completion</td>
          <td><monospace>Completion</monospace></td>
          <td>(<xref alt="Keshavan et al., 2010" rid="ref-Keshavan2010" ref-type="bibr">Keshavan
          et al., 2010</xref>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Largest Gap algorithm</td>
          <td><monospace>LG</monospace></td>
          <td>(<xref alt="Channarond et al., 2012" rid="ref-channarond2012" ref-type="bibr">Channarond
          et al., 2012</xref>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sort and Smooth</td>
          <td><monospace>SAS</monospace></td>
          <td>(<xref alt="Chan &amp; Airoldi, 2014" rid="ref-chan2014" ref-type="bibr">Chan
          &amp; Airoldi, 2014</xref>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Network Histogram</td>
          <td><monospace>HistogramEstimator</monospace></td>
          <td>(<xref alt="Olhede &amp; Wolfe, 2014" rid="ref-olhede2014" ref-type="bibr">Olhede
          &amp; Wolfe, 2014</xref>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Neighborhood smoothing</td>
          <td><monospace>NBDsmooth</monospace></td>
          <td>(<xref alt="Zhang et al., 2017" rid="ref-Zhang2015" ref-type="bibr">Zhang
